      {
        "id": "upstream-latitude-16-task-scheduler",
        "revision": "fixture@a96b805e722f4d63",
        "repo": ".ops/benchmark-repos/latitude-dev--coding-agent-benchmark",
        "baseCommit": "cd28f3965d4d9d2fc9a49faca573f16b5102b488",
        "harness": "native",
        "goal": "Fix the scheduler ordering in src/order.js so a newly ready high-priority task runs as soon as its dependency finishes, and the frozen upstream suite passes.",
        "prompt": "# Bug report: high-priority tasks run last once their dependency finishes\n\nWe use this scheduler to run build pipelines. Tasks declare `dependsOn` and a numeric\n`priority`, and the scheduler promises that whenever several tasks are ready to run at\nthe same moment, the one with the highest priority goes first (ties break alphabetically\nby name).\n\nThat promise is broken. With these tasks:\n\n- `fetch` (priority 5)\n- `transform` (priority 4, depends on `fetch`)\n- `archive` (priority 2)\n- `cleanup` (priority 1)\n\nthe run order should be `fetch, transform, archive, cleanup`, because the moment `fetch`\nfinishes, `transform` is ready and outranks everything else. Instead we observe\n`fetch, archive, cleanup, transform`, so our most urgent follow-up work waits behind\nlow-priority housekeeping. The recorded run log after a failed task shows the same\nmisordering, which breaks our reporting.\n\nThe test suite in this workspace reproduces the problem. Find the bug in the source and\nfix it so the whole suite passes.\n\n---\nExecution workspace (this is an isolated checkout of the upstream benchmark):\n- Fix only `tasks/16-task-scheduler/workspace/src/`.\n- The frozen test is `tasks/16-task-scheduler/workspace/scheduler.test.js`; run it with:\n  node --test --test-reporter=tap tasks/16-task-scheduler/workspace/scheduler.test.js\n- Do not edit the test file or `package.json`, and do not weaken the tests.\n- Report what you changed and the test result.\n",
